## Conclusion

Burnout is more than feeling tired after a demanding week. It can involve ongoing exhaustion, growing disconnection from work, and a reduced sense of professional effectiveness.

Recognizing these experiences is an important step toward understanding what needs to change.

Practical adjustments such as setting realistic boundaries, taking breaks, reconnecting with supportive people, and identifying workplace stressors may help. However, self-care alone cannot solve every problem, particularly when the working environment continues to create excessive or unmanageable demands.

If you are struggling with emotional exhaustion or ongoing work-related stress, therapy may provide a supportive place to understand your experience and explore healthier ways forward.

You do not have to wait until you reach your breaking point to ask for help. Your well-being matters just as much as your responsibilities.

### Mental Health Disclaimer

This article is intended for general educational purposes and is not a substitute for professional mental health care, medical advice, or diagnosis. Burnout is an occupational phenomenon, and symptoms such as fatigue, sleep difficulties, anxiety, or low mood can have different causes. A qualified healthcare or mental health professional can help assess your concerns and discuss appropriate support.

If you are experiencing thoughts of suicide or self-harm, seek immediate support. In the United States, call or text 988 to reach the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline. If you are in immediate danger or experiencing a life-threatening emergency, call 911.

